@@ -0,0 +1,29 @@
+# The Daily Grind
+
+Run `pnpm start` to serve the site, or `npm test` to run the Node integration tests.
+
+## WebMCP
+
+`webmcp.js` registers three client-side tools when the browser provides
+`document.modelContext` (or the older `navigator.modelContext`):
+
+| Tool | Input | Behavior |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `get_seasonal_favorites` | `{}` | Returns names, descriptions, and detail paths from the visible cards. |
+| `get_site_navigation` | `{}` | Returns labeled navigation and coffee detail paths, excluding logout. |
+| `navigate_site` | `{ "path": "/menu" }` | Navigates to an exact, same-origin path discovered by the read tools. |
+
+Tools read the same DOM content used by visitors. Navigation only follows existing
+links; it does not implement their destination pages, submit orders, or log out.
+Schemas describe accepted inputs, and handlers also validate inputs at runtime.
+Registration failure is handled without interrupting the page. Tools are removed
+on page exit and registered again on back/forward cache restoration.
+
+WebMCP requires a supporting browser and a secure context (HTTPS or localhost).
+Unsupported browsers retain the regular website without a polyfill. No server-side
+MCP endpoint or AI service is required. API reference:
+[Chrome WebMCP imperative API](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/ai/webmcp/imperative-api).
+
+Tests use the actual HTML with DOM and browser API stubs to check tool registration,
+returned content, input validation, navigation, unsupported browsers, registration
+failure, and page lifecycle cleanup. They do not launch a browser.
